WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.130
Film Courage: You’re saying the interrogation
process works when there’s another person

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.150
interrogating them?

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.150
Mark W. Travis: Yes.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.730
Film Courage: Let’s take something where…slightly
different scenario…maybe I am making a different

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:18.350
way around it in that…let’s take a movie
like THE REVENANT and let’s take Hugh Glass’s

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.490
character before the bear attack.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.440
He’s alone in the forest.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:31.220
How are you interrogating Hugh Glass to stumble
across the bear or encountering it in some

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.220
way?

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.430
How are you getting him ready for this scene?

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.050
Mark W. Travis: Okay…first I am just getting
back to what you said in the beginning about

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.300
interrogating involves someone else.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.820
The interrogation process is usually between
someone who is not in the scene and the person

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:44.840
who is in the scene.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.120
So whether there is one person in the scene
or 12 people makes no difference.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.170
Each actor needs that other person and it’s
usually someone who is not in the scene.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:59.350
But getting to how do you interrogate someone
to play a scene like Hugh Glass’s scene

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.869
where he is going through the forest (the
woods) and then he’s attacked by a bear.

00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:11.639
One thing you’ve got to be very clear about,
the interrogation is pretty simple yet profound

00:01:11.639 --> 00:01:12.740
for a moment like that.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:17.490
But first of all you’ve got to remember
in any scene, the character that you are interrogating

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.539
does not know what’s coming up in the scene.

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:23.950
This is a problem between the character and
actor.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:24.969
The actor knows.

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:28.810
Leonardo DiCaprio, he knows exactly what’s
going to happen.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:30.270
There is going to be a bear and he’s going
to have to fight it off.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.279
Got it!

00:01:31.279 --> 00:01:33.590
The character doesn’t know that.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:34.850
This is the power of interrogation.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:41.249
I’ve got to get his mind back into the character’s
mind and away from the actor’s mind.

00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:46.200
What the actor knows (this is a little detour),
what the actor knows what’s coming up in

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:53.020
the scene, the lines I have to say, what I
have to do, that’s all there in the background,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.020
okay.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.490
It’s not driving anything because it can’t
drive it, because it’s not known by the

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.520
character.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.590
I will trust that it will always be there.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.130
I will trust that the actors will always say
their lines.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:10.950
They’ll follow the stage and they’ll do
the mechanics.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:15.569
So it’s not the mechanics I am worried about,
I’m worried about the mindset of the characters.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:23.129
So interrogating Hugh Glass before the bear,
I have to know and so does Hugh Glass have

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:25.739
to know, what the hell are you doing?

00:02:25.739 --> 00:02:26.849
What are you doing?

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:29.879
Where are you going into and what are you
expecting?

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:34.640
Now in the interrogation I could say okay
I could be talking to him where are you going.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.640
Okay you’ve got to go.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.640
You’re going to get that?

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.540
Let’s say he’s looking for food!

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.670
I’m just making this up because I don’t
recall what he was doing, he’s just moving

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.530
but Hugh Glass knows what he’s doing!

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:53.049
Even if we look at the scene we’re not sure…he
knows what he’s doing and through the interrogation

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:58.859
I am going to plant in his mind or inject
or question and we’re going to establish

00:02:58.859 --> 00:03:04.530
through me and Hugh Glass what he’s doing,
what he’s looking for, right?

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:10.269
Now through that interrogation I could start
to insinuate potential danger, you know?

00:03:10.269 --> 00:03:11.269
“You’re all alone.

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:12.269
You’re all alone out here.

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:13.750
I mean, what if something happens?”

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:21.810
Now he knows as the actor I’m setting the
character for what the environment and what’s

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.010
going on and actually setting the character
up for the scene.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.209
The actor knows that because there is a bear
coming.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:30.280
Hugh Glass is just saying “Yeah, I know
I’m staying but I’m fine.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.280
I’ve got my rifle.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.280
I’m fine.”

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.840
I said “You sure?

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.499
Could you handle something…what if an Elk
comes?”

00:03:37.499 --> 00:03:45.939
I want to get him into our mindset, a specific
mindset of my choice.

00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:48.269
The mindset could be “I’m fine.

00:03:48.269 --> 00:03:50.689
I’m in no danger at all.”

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:52.010
That’s one mindset!

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.579
Another mindset is “I know I’m out here
alone and I shouldn’t be and this is the

00:03:55.579 --> 00:03:57.180
stupidest thing you should ever do.”

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.709
That’s a different mindset!

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:00.520
Where do I want to take him?

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:04.379
What mindset do I want to put him in?

00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:10.459
I can set up that mindset and then the beauty
of interrogation is you work with the character,

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:15.709
you’re interrogating the character and you
set up a mindset, a belief system and attitude

00:04:15.709 --> 00:04:16.709
just before the scene.

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:20.350
A lot of actors will talk about that moment
before the scene where what they are thinking

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.509
about just before the scene, the interrogation.

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:29.630
I can create that, anything I want and then
what I do (which I love) is then I send them

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.500
into the scene naive.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:38.080
An actor will go into the scene with a plan.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.160
This is disastrous because when’s the last
time you went into a 3-minute moment in your

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.340
life with a plan knowing what you were going
to say and what you were going to do and how

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.870
you were going to react.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.389
We don’t do that!

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:54.449
That’s not authentic, “It’s acting.”

00:04:54.449 --> 00:05:01.360
My job is to send my character into a scene
naive having no idea what’s going to come

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.389
on because I want the genuine authentic reactions
to be events that happen to the other lines

00:05:07.389 --> 00:05:13.110
that are said, to the attack of the bear,
to the realization that he’s been stupid…whatever.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.770
I want it to happen authentically.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:17.110
I don’t want him to plan it.

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.780
The actor will try to plan it.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.460
The character can’t plant it.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:25.699
So if I get him in the mind of the character
believing something totally different than

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:31.509
what’s going to happen then he will react
to every single moment authentically and it

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:35.050
will probably surprise him and it will probably
surprise me.

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:37.729
The guarantees I can make to every director.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:46.300
If you work this way and you send a character
into a scene, naive without a plan, knowing

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.330
that yes the actor will say the right lines
and do the blocking and do all of that.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:53.110
But without a plan for the emotional journey
he’s going to go through.

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:58.771
How he’s going to react to anything with
no plan I’m guaranteed number one: the actor

00:05:58.771 --> 00:06:02.800
will not know what he’s going to do…at
all.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.659
Certainly the character doesn’t know because
the character is just going through it.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:12.409
Actor one: you will not know what he’s going
to do but everything he does will be authentic

00:06:12.409 --> 00:06:18.090
and that’s what we’re going for and then
you get to choose in post-production as we’ve

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:24.250
talked about before which moment and then
when you do take two you set him up slightly

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.960
differently, a different mindset.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.979
Maybe a more cautious mindset this time for
Hugh Glass.

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:36.600
Maybe a mindset of deriding himself “You
stupid, okay what are you doing out here?”

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.539
Trying to convince himself he’s going to
be okay and he shouldn’t be out there.

00:06:39.539 --> 00:06:41.720
He will react differently then.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.910
If you do it three or four takes like that
and he reacts differently and you’ve shot

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:50.630
them all, you realize the range of material
you’ve got and it’s all authentic.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.530
You can build the most beautiful scene possible.

